## Looking Forward to Next Week

Building good infrastructure to support a big project is hard, and we didn't do
it near well enough in Rubinius before.

I've been building out better capabilities piece-by-piece over the past month.
For Rubinius, these include
[project](https://github.com/orgs/rubinius/projects),
[docs](https://rubinius.com/docs/), and the [blog](https://rubinius.com/blog/).
And [the same](https://github.com/orgs/vivarium-ai/projects) [for]([docs/)
[Vivarium](/blog/), including [these newsletters](/newsletters/).

GitHub Projects has a neat feature where you can enable an automation that will
add issues tagged with a certain label to the project. This makes it easy to
add an issue, tag it, and the relevant project(s) update automatically.

The roadmaps for [Rubinius](https://rubinius.com/docs/articles/roadmap/) and
[Vivarium](/docs/articles/roadmap/) are now in the docs directly, and will be
updated and improved so they are always easily accessible to contributors. It's
always important for roadmaps to stay current with changing circumstances, so
graphical representations with planned versus completed work will be coming
soon.

Now that the basic proof-of-concept of the new build system appears to be
working, the goal is to get [legacy
Rubinius](https://github.com/rubinius/rubinius) working in parallel with the
[rbx compiler](https://github.com/rubinius/rbx) so that the Ruby core library
can be updated to the [soon-to-be-released Ruby
4.0](https://www.ruby-lang.org/en/news/2025/11/17/ruby-4-0-0-preview2-released/)
while the compiler is built out.
